In the beginning days, the Mountain stood tall and proud, holding vast reserves of snow and ice within its peaks. Below lived the River, who flowed through the valleys bringing life to all creatures.

One scorching summer, the River began to run dry. She approached the Mountain with a humble request: "Share your snow with me, that I might continue to nourish the land."

The Mountain pondered this. "If I release my snow to you, how do I know you will not simply flow away to distant seas, leaving my slopes barren?"

The River replied, "What I receive, I will return threefold. The waters I carry will rise as mist, forming clouds that will bless you with rain and replenish your snows."

The Mountain decided to trust. He released his melting snow, which flowed into the River, tripling her strength. True to her word, the River's waters evaporated under the sun, formed clouds, and returned to the Mountain as rain—not just once, but season after season.

And so they learned: trust given is trust multiplied, and reciprocity sustained becomes the foundation of prosperity.

From that day forward, Mountain and River have maintained their sacred cycle, each trusting the other to fulfill their part.
